#
# prima-materia — fifteen named cases with an interval
#
# What the section of testimonials is for the examination layer: the only place
# in this corpus where a claim carries a person, a township, a year and a
# stated interval. Everything else in the handbook is an instruction.
# See prima-materia#523.
#

pmp:HohmanTestimonialsExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Examination of the twenty-four hour interval in the testimonials"@en, "Prüfung der Vierundzwanzig-Stunden-Frist der Zeugnisse"@de ;
    pm:tests pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchussblatterHeilung ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An application of the kind the section reports after which the complaint persists beyond the interval the section states for it, that is beyond a little more than twenty-four hours for the eye and the limb, beyond seven to nine hours for the erysipelas on the cheek, and beyond the following morning for the erysipelas on the finger."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-13" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-13" ;
    skos:note "The interval is what makes the section examinable, and it is the transmission's own. Eleven of the fifteen cases give one: a little more than twenty-four hours, in a short time, in some seven or nine hours, by the following morning. A claim with a deadline can be missed; a claim of healing without one cannot."@en ;
    skos:note "Against it stands the base rate, and it is large. A shot-blister on the eye, a sore finger, a headache and a mouth sore remit without treatment, most of them within a day. The section reports no case that failed and gives no denominator, so what it offers is fifteen successes out of an unknown number of applications. That is the condition under which the state stays at procedure without cases: a series of confirmations with no count of the attempts is not a set of cases."@en ;
    skos:note "Two of the fifteen are not the author's own work. The boy of Johann Bingeman, in whom mortification had already set in, and the erysipelas on Bingeman's wife were both treated by the author's wife; the case of Jacob Staufer's child was performed by Staufer himself after his neighbour urged it. The last is the strongest of the fifteen for the transmission rather than for the man, because there the book worked in a hand that was not its author's."@en ;
    skos:note "The mortification case is the one that could not be explained by remission and is the one that ought to be checked first, if any record of it survives. It is also the case the preface is written against, since the preface pronounces a woe on whoever lets a man lose life or limb to mortification when this booklet could have helped."@en ;
    skos:note "No counter-search state, because no evidence from outside the transmission has been taken in. The counter-search here would be the record of Berks and Bucks County between eighteen twelve and eighteen nineteen, which nobody has opened for these names."@en .
